Naming and visual identity
--------------------------
The application is called **Spark Game Launcher**. The name deliberately
contains no NVIDIA trademark -- no "DGX", "RTX", "NVIDIA", "CUDA", "GeForce" or
"Blackwell" -- and the word "Spark" is used descriptively, of the class of
machine the software targets.

The application deliberately does NOT imitate NVIDIA's visual identity:

  * It uses none of NVIDIA's logos, wordmarks, icons or product imagery.
  * It uses NO NVIDIA green. The colour appears nowhere in the application.
  * Its palette is gold plus arcade neon (blue, purple, pink), chosen to read as
    a gaming utility rather than as a vendor product.

This is a deliberate design decision, recorded because it is the point:
NVIDIA's brand-usage guidance asks third parties not to mimic NVIDIA's branding
and visual style. This project follows that, in substance rather than in a
footnote.

If NVIDIA, HP, Valve or Canonical would like anything here changed or removed,
open an issue -- it will be addressed promptly and in good faith.

Why this project exists
-----------------------
Everything here depends on NVIDIA's product ecosystem. There is no version of
this work that does not: the hardware, the driver, the CUDA and Vulkan stacks,
and the DGX OS image are all NVIDIA's or built in partnership with NVIDIA. That
dependency is stated plainly rather than glossed over, because it is the honest
description of the relationship.

The DGX Spark is a fixed, ubiquitous platform sold as an AI developer machine.
It also happens to be a capable piece of gaming hardware that almost nobody has
characterised for that purpose. This project exists so that a person who owns
one can find out what actually runs on it, and why -- without repeating a week
of somebody else's dead ends.

How that dependency is honoured
-------------------------------
1. NVIDIA's intellectual property is respected. No logos, wordmarks, product
   imagery or brand colours are used. Trademarks are used nominatively, to name
   the things being tested, and never in this project's own name or identity.

2. What this is and why it exists is described in writing, in the open, here and
   in the README -- so nobody has to infer intent from behaviour.

3. Findings are reported accurately, and that includes unflattering ones.
   Respect is not silence. When something in the stack is broken, this project
   writes a minimal reproducer, checks it against a working control, and files
   it upstream. The bug reports in notes/upstream/ are the most useful thing
   this project can offer the ecosystem it depends on, and they are offered in
   that spirit: specific, evidence-backed, and free of editorialising about
   whose fault it is.

4. Claims are held to evidence. Overstating what this hardware can do would be a
   disservice to NVIDIA and to the reader; so would understating it. Several
   published conclusions here have been retracted when the evidence did not
   hold, and those retractions are left visible in the log on purpose.

5. No endorsement is claimed or implied, and none has been sought or given.
